call site verbatim (formatWholeDollars in quick-adjust-scripts.html; the
phase-note cents line in portfolio-settings.html; the three healthcare-person.html
oninput handlers). All produced en-US-grouped output ($7,387 / $9,233 / $8,124.60
etc.) under the simulated de-DE environment — the drift is dead on every dollar
string this run touched, because every call now takes an explicit 'en-US' (or
'en-US', {...}) argument rather than the OS/browser default.

(2) Whole-app sweep: `grep -rn "toLocaleString" web/` returns exactly 5 hits, all
in whatif templates (quick-adjust-scripts.html:12, portfolio-settings.html:170,
healthcare-person.html:81/121/148) and all pin 'en-US'. No hits in
dashboard.js/whatif-tabs.js/whatif-poll.js. charts.js uses `Intl.NumberFormat('en-US', ...)`
(already locale-pinned, untouched by this run). `grep -rnE
"toLocaleString\s*\(\s*\)"` and the `undefined`-locale variant both return zero
matches app-wide (whitespace-obfuscated bare calls also ruled out). No other
same-page server/client disagreement introduced by this run.

(3) Intl edge/boundary probe: compared Go's canonical half-away-from-zero
formatter against `Math.round(v).toLocaleString('en-US')` for 0, 0.4, 0.5, 999,
999.5, 1000, 999999.5, 1e9, 1234567890.5, 1e-7 — all match exactly, including
comma-grouping at the billion boundary. Found two non-shipping edge notes, not
failures: (a) the oracle's own throwaway `zzCanonWhole` helper in accept.sh
mis-places the comma for negative inputs ("$-,100") — a cosmetic bug in the test
helper itself, not in the production `internal/templates/render.go
formatWholeDollars`, which correctly strips sign before grouping and returns
"-$100"; (b) JS Math.round(-0.5).toLocaleString('en-US') yields "$-0" vs Go's
"$0" — but living expenses and every other value routed through
formatWholeDollars on this page are non-negative by construction (multipliers,
slider minimums all >= 0), so this is unreachable in the app, not a same-page
divergence.

(4) Node-harness load-order check: real page script order in whatif.html is
`whatif-spending-preview-scripts` (defines updateSpendingPreview) BEFORE
`whatif-quick-adjust-scripts` (defines formatWholeDollars) — the opposite of
what the render_spending_preview_test.go comment claims ("the same load order as
the real page"; the harness concatenates quick-adjust-scripts first). This
comment is factually wrong. It does not mask a real defect: verified by swapping
the concatenation order in a scratch copy of the test (m+qa instead of qa+m) —
TestUpdateSpendingPreview_NoPanelDoesNotThrow still passes either way, because
both are plain top-level `function` declarations with no immediate/top-level
cross-calls; every real invocation (oninput/onclick) fires only after the full
page — hence all script tags — has already parsed and executed. Confirmed no
DOMContentLoaded listener anywhere in rate-assumptions.html or
quick-adjust-scripts.html calls updateSpendingPreview or formatWholeDollars at
load time. Traced the actual template inclusion chain
(whatif.html:101 whatif-rate-assumptions inputs, :155-159 script includes,
:159 quick-adjust-scripts) to confirm this. Verdict: comment inaccuracy, not a
functional defect — noted for the record, not grounds for FAIL.
